Module
ARCOS	—	Specification,	Testing	&	Governance	Framework
What	a	Module	Is
A	Module	is	governed,	reusable,	composable,	evolvable
infrastructure	—	not	a	collection	of	capabilities	bolted	onto	a
contract.
It	is	one	coherent	domain	of	a	digital	asset’s	behavior,	built	once	as
reusable	machinery	instead	of	welded	per-project	into	each	NFT
contract.
Definition:	A	Module	is	a	governed	state	machine	—	one	structured
domain	of	state,	owned	by	exactly	one	authority,	changed	only
through	defined	transitions,	reachable	by	the	rest	of	the	system
only	through	MAS.
A	capability	is	what	a	Module	can	do.	The	Module	is	the	machinery
that	determines	how,	when,	and	under	whose	authority	that
capability	operates.
The	Problem	This	Solves
Fragmentation	—	capability	logic	for	an	NFT	(delegation,	custody,
rental,	verification,	royalty	enforcement,	and	so	on)	is	scattered
across	separate	ERC	extensions	and	custom	code,	each	solving	one
piece	with	no	shared	coordination	between	them.	The	ERCs	and
extensions	themselves	are	sound	—	the	gap	is	that	nothing
coordinates	them	once	a	project	adopts	more	than	one.
Custom-built,	one-off	models	—	every	project	re-derives	its	own
logic	for	how	these	capabilities	should	behave,	from	scratch,
because	there	is	no	reusable	governed	machinery	to	build	on
instead.
Frozen,	not	evolvable	—	once	a	contract	is	deployed,	that	custom
logic	is	fixed.	Adding	a	new	capability	later,	or	changing	how	an
existing	one	behaves,	isn’t	possible	without	a	new	deployment	and
a	migration.
Not	reusable	—	the	coordination	logic	one	project	writes	to	make
its	capabilities	work	together	cannot	be	picked	up	and	reused	by
the	next	project.	Every	project	pays	this	cost	again,	independently.
Not	composable	—	capabilities	built	as	bespoke,	project-specific
code	don’t	interoperate.	There’s	no	shared	layer	for	two
capabilities	on	the	same	token	to	coordinate	through	when	they
collide.
The	Six	Fixed	Questions
Every	Module	answers	the	same	six	architectural	questions.	The
questions	are	universal;	the	answers	and	mechanisms	are	domain
specific.
1.	State	/	Structure	—	what	the	Module	owns,	and	what	that	state
means
2.	Authority	—	who	can	cause	that	state	to	change
3.	Transitions	—	how	the	state	legitimately	changes
4.	Invariants	/	Rules	—	what	must	hold	true	before	and	after	a	change
5.	Lifecycle	&	History	/	Provenance	—	how	the	state	exists	and
evolves	over	time
6.	System	Interaction	—	how	the	rest	of	ARCOS	reads	and	triggers
this	state,	exclusively	through	MAS
What	Is	Not	Universal
Beyond	the	six	fixed	questions,	a	Module	may	adopt	additional
mechanisms	—	recovery	paths,	versioning,	staged/two-phase
transitions,	time-bound	state,	sub-delegation	of	authority,	and
others	—	but	only	where	its	own	domain	actually	has	that	need.
These	mechanisms	are	not	layers	every	Module	must	implement.
They	are	optional	answers	to	the	six	questions,	not	additional
questions.
Whether	a	Module	needs	any	given	mechanism	is	decided	by	its
domain,	not	by	precedent	from	another	Module.	One	Module
needing	a	recovery	path	doesn’t	imply	the	next	one	does.
Example:	a	Module	whose	state	is	issued	and	later	invalidated	(an
attestation,	a	certification)	may	need	no	recovery	mechanism	at	all
—	an	invalid	record	is	revoked,	not	recovered.	A	Module	whose
state	represents	a	claim	that	can	be	lost	or	contested	may	need
one.	Both	are	correct	for	their	own	domain.
The	Four	Properties
The	four	properties	are	the	outcome	of	answering	the	six	questions
well	—	not	four	inputs	that	generate	the	six	questions.
Governed	—	the	whole	architecture	operates	through	the	state
machine;	no	path	around	it.
Reusable	—	any	NFT	can	adopt	the	Module	without	assembling
custom	logic,	subject	to	trusting	whatever	currently	executes	MAS.
Composable	—	Modules	connect	to	each	other	only	through	MAS;
no	Module	calls	another	directly.
Evolvable	—	capabilities	and	mechanisms	evolve;	the	governed
state	model	and	the	six-question	architecture	stay	constant
underneath	that	evolution.
MAS:	The	Only	Path	Between
Modules
No	Module	ever	calls	another	Module	directly.
Routing	—	MAS	is	the	only	entry	point	into	a	Module.
Aggregation	—	when	an	operation	needs	input	from	multiple
Modules,	MAS	collects	each	one’s	check	and	returns	a	single
decision;	no	Module	needs	to	know	the	others	exist.
No	duplicate	state	—	if	one	Module’s	state	answers	a	question
another	Module	needs,	the	second	queries	the	first	through	MAS
instead	of	holding	its	own	copy.
Status
This	framework	has	been	validated	against	one	domain:
Ownership.
The	other	planned	Modules	(Transfer,	Permission,	Metadata,
Verification,	Royalty,	Composition,	Security)	are	scoped	at	this
framework	but	not	yet	built	out	to	the	same	depth.
Composable	and	Evolvable	are	demonstrated	as	design	intent;	they
have	not	yet	been	proven	across	more	than	one	Module.
See	Ownership.md	for	the	framework	worked	through	in	full
